// Both binding arrows mean the same thing; where a name lands is decided by the script's
// `+scope` mode. This script sets none, so it runs under the default, `+scope:global`: a
// binding is visible to the block that made it, to that block's siblings, and to everything
// they nest. No wrapper `using` is needed for that — the top level is a scope like any other.
// The three `await`s use staggered durations so one clock advance fires each exactly once,
// in the order written.

await 1:00
    `checked in` => shift_note
    POST `first: ${ shift_note }`

await 1:10
    // Visible: the block above is a sibling of this one.
    POST `second: ${ shift_note }`

    `also shared` -> other_note

    if #live is true
        POST `nested: ${ other_note }`

await 1:20
    // Never bound anywhere. Reading it is not an error — it is empty, exactly as `.prop`
    // on a subject that has no such property is empty.
    POST `missing: [${ never_bound }]`

    // `->` and `=>` are synonyms, so the `->` binding above is shared too.
    POST `sibling: ${ other_note }`
